From 9ce26fed69abc05d013526c0d26dc420983c9480 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Destrudos6 <3411622425@qq.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 10:57:46 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 30/51] Add cloud backup to user-owned WebDAV or S3 storage

Baking on the research of remotely-save (the popular Obsidian sync
plugin) and its backend abstraction, whose free self-hosted core
covers exactly WebDAV (Nextcloud, Synology, NutStore, AList, ...)
and S3 (MinIO, Cloudflare R2, Backblaze B2, AWS, ...): those two
backends are implemented on plain OkHttp with zero new
dependencies, including a hand-rolled AWS SigV4 signer with
path-style addressing and an XML parser for ListObjectsV2, and
PROPFIND/MKCOL/PUT/GET/DELETE with Basic auth for WebDAV.

A new second-level settings page (backup & restore -> cloud
backup) configures the backend, tests the connection, uploads a
complete backup zip on demand (database, settings, bisub subtitle
cache - the same content as the manual export) and restores any
remote backup with a picker, confirmation and an app restart.

Automatic backups run through a periodic WorkManager job (daily
or weekly) that fails silently and retries on the next run; the
retention setting prunes the oldest remote backups after each
upload. The configuration lives in the default shared
preferences, so a restored device reconnects to its storage
automatically.

ContentSettingsManager learns to export into a plain OutputStream
and to import from a local zip file, which is what the cloud
pipeline uses; the SAF-based manual export/import keeps working
through the same core.
